From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the SBU edge from the DisplayPort transmitter to the receptacle

Add the graph edge that `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-tcon-dp-edge` said it did not have. `edp0` gains `port@1` and the
`usb-c-connector` gains `port@2`, and between them they are what lets
sun60i-dp.c find the receptacle's device_node -- the node an out-of-band
hot-plug is matched against, and the one thing missing between a Type-C alt
mode that enters and a connector that never says `connected`.

WHY port@2 AND NOT SOMETHING INVENTED
--------------------------------------

usb-connector.yaml fixes the numbering, and the SBU port is the one it names for
exactly this:

      port@0:  High Speed (HS), present in all connectors.
      port@1:  Super Speed (SS), present in SS capable connectors.
      port@2:  Sideband Use (SBU), present in USB-C. This describes the
               alternate mode connection of which SBU is a part.
    required:
      - port@0
              -- Documentation/devicetree/bindings/connector/usb-connector.yaml:195-212

and its own worked example wires that port to a DisplayPort AUX endpoint on a
board built the same way as this one -- "HS lines routed to companion PMIC, SS
lines to USB3 PHY and SBU to DisplayPort":

        port@2 {
            reg = <2>;
            usb_con_sbu: endpoint {
                remote-endpoint = <&dp_aux>;
            };
        };
              -- ibid:418-424

That is what this board is: DP-AUXP/AUXN go straight to SBU1/SBU2 with no
external mux and no redriver (`sch p12 L19-44`), while the four main lanes leave through COMB0 and
are already described by the connector's `port@1`. So the SBU edge is a true
description of the wiring, not a software hook wearing a graph edge's clothes.

⚠ IT CARRIES NO VIDEO, and the comment in the node says so, because the next
reader will otherwise assume port@2 is where the picture goes.

THE ALTERNATIVE, AND WHY IT IS REFUSED
---------------------------------------

`displayport = <&edp0>` on the receptacle would also work -- dp_altmode_probe()
prefers it over the port's own fwnode (displayport.c:800-804) -- and it is one
line instead of two ports. It is refused because the property is in the code and
in NO binding: usb-connector.yaml is `unevaluatedProperties: false` and
documents only an `altmodes/displayport` CHILD NODE, which is not a property,
and no device tree in 6.18.36 sets it. Writing it would mean inventing a
property, which is the failure that grepping the driver for the property
is meant to prevent, one step further on: here somebody DOES read it, but no
schema accepts it. `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-tcon-dp-edge`'s paragraph recording that debt is deleted rather than
struck through, and replaced by the edge that settles it.

WHAT ELSE READS THESE PORTS -- checked, not assumed
----------------------------------------------------

Following the same "grep the driver for it" check: a new endpoint on the connector is
walked by the Type-C core when it looks for a mux and for an orientation
switch: `fwnode_connection_find_match()` iterates every endpoint of the
connector and hands each remote port parent to `typec_mux_match()` /
`typec_switch_match()`. Both return NULL unless the remote node carries
`mode-switch` / `orientation-switch` respectively
(drivers/usb/typec/mux.c, both matchers open with that test). `edp0` carries
neither -- only `combophy0` does, from `arm64-dts-a733-enable-dp-alt-mode-negotiation` -- so the new edge is invisible to
that search and cannot displace the existing mux. Nothing else in
`drivers/usb/typec/` walks the connector's graph.

On the other side, `of_graph_get_remote_node(edp0, 1, 0)` resolves through
`of_graph_get_remote_port_parent()` -> `of_graph_get_port_parent()`, which skips
the `ports` container and returns the `connector` node itself
(drivers/of/property.c) -- which is precisely the node that
`device_get_named_child_node(tcpc_dev, "connector")` (tcpci.c:822) handed to
TCPM, and therefore the fwnode `dp_altmode_probe()` will match on.

fw_devlink: A NEW EDGE IS A NEW DEPENDENCY, AND THIS ONE IS A CYCLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------

`remote-endpoint` is one of fw_devlink's supplier bindings
(drivers/of/property.c:1514-1518, `parse_remote_endpoint` returning
`of_graph_get_remote_port_parent()` with `.get_con_dev =
of_graph_get_port_parent`), so this edge makes `edp0` a consumer of the device
that owns the receptacle -- the I2C TCPC, because the `connector` node itself
binds nothing. Taken alone that would be the very coupling ``drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training`` refuses: the
DisplayPort transmitter waiting on the Type-C controller.

It is not taken alone. The edge is described from both ends, as every OF graph
edge is, so the receptacle is equally a consumer of `edp0`, and fw_devlink
resolves the pair as a cycle: `__fw_devlink_relax_cycles()` marks both links
FWLINK_FLAG_CYCLE and `fw_devlink_get_flags()` then downgrades them to
FW_DEVLINK_FLAGS_PERMISSIVE | DL_FLAG_CYCLE (drivers/base/core.c:1706-1712,
:1969-1990, :2139-2146), which orders nothing and gates no probe. The same shape
already runs on this board: `edp0` <-> `tcon_tv1` (`arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-tcon-dp-edge`) and `usb_con` <->
`combophy0` (`arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c1-husb311-typec`) are both bidirectional and both probe.

⬜ Observable, and it needs no display and no monitor: the boot log gains
`Fixed dependency cycle(s) with ...` naming `displayport@5740000` and the
connector (core.c:2143-2144). Refuted if `5740000.displayport` instead
appears in /sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred. ⚠ Even then the failure would be
bounded -- the transmitter would probe after the TCPC rather than never -- which
is why this is a note and not a blocker.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES INSTEAD
-----------------------------

The vendor's board DT describes no such edge, because its driver does not walk
one: `sunxi_drm_edp.c:3406` takes its hot-plug source from
`extcon_get_edev_by_phandle(dev, 0)`, i.e. an `extcon` phandle to a producer for
`EXTCON_DISP_DP`. Mainline 6.18.36 has two such producers -- a Rockchip PHY and
the ChromeOS EC -- and none in `drivers/usb/typec/`, so that phandle has nothing
to point at on a mainline stack. The graph edge replaces it and describes real
wiring while doing so, which the phandle did not.

⬜ No binding schema, and no binding id: no display node in this tree has one,
and this patch adds neither a clock nor a reset.

WHAT IS VERIFIED
-----------------

✅ Applies at --fuzz=0 as part of the full series on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36.
✅ The dtb builds and carries both endpoints and their phandle pair.
⬜ NOT HW-PROVEN. A graph edge writes no register; on its own it changes
   nothing, and without ``drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training`` nothing walks it.

THE FALSIFIER
--------------

It has none of its own -- its observable is ``drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training``'s: with the monitor plugged
into J4, `/sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status` reads `connected`, and it changes on
unplug and on re-plug, with no debugfs verb written. ⚠ Not `/dev/fb0`, and not a
picture: this hardware trains no link until the pixel path is up, which is a separate defect with a separate fix.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1743,23 +1743,30 @@
 			status = "okay";
 
 			/*
-			 * The input edge, TCON_TV1 -> this transmitter. Only the
-			 * other end is walked -- of_graph_get_remote_node() is
-			 * called on the TCON -- but a graph edge exists only if
-			 * both ends describe it, so this half is not optional.
+			 * Two edges: TCON_TV1 in on port@0, the Type-C
+			 * receptacle out on port@1.
 			 *
-			 * ⬜ AND THE CONNECTOR IS STILL NOT DESCRIBED HERE. The
-			 * Type-C receptacle's `displayport` property, which is
-			 * what would let dp_altmode_probe() find this node's
-			 * connector for out-of-band hot-plug, is in
-			 * displayport.c:800-804 and in NO binding:
-			 * usb-connector.yaml is `unevaluatedProperties: false`
-			 * and documents only an `altmodes/displayport` CHILD
-			 * NODE, which does not satisfy that lookup. `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` records
-			 * the same debt from the driver side. It is closed by one
-			 * observation once a connector exists -- plug the monitor
-			 * and read /sys/class/drm/<card>-DP-1/status -- and not
-			 * by adding a property no schema accepts.
+			 * The input half is walked only from the other end --
+			 * of_graph_get_remote_node() is called on the TCON --
+			 * but a graph edge exists only if both ends describe
+			 * it, so this half is not optional.
+			 *
+			 * The output half is what closes the hot-plug path, and
+			 * it is described here rather than with a `displayport`
+			 * property on the receptacle because that property is in
+			 * displayport.c:801 and in NO binding: usb-connector.yaml
+			 * is `unevaluatedProperties: false` and documents only an
+			 * `altmodes/displayport` CHILD NODE, which is not a
+			 * property. This driver walks the edge instead
+			 * (sun60i_dp_next_bridge_get()), publishes a
+			 * drm_aux_hpd_bridge on the node it lands on, and chains
+			 * it behind its own -- so drm_bridge_connector_init()
+			 * takes the RECEPTACLE's fwnode, which is the one
+			 * dp_altmode_probe() looks the connector up by.
+			 *
+			 * It carries no video. SBU is two AUX wires; the four
+			 * main lanes leave through COMB0 and are described by
+			 * port@1 of the connector, not this edge.
 			 */
 			ports {
 				#address-cells = <1>;
@@ -1772,6 +1779,14 @@
 						remote-endpoint = <&tcon_tv1_out_edp0>;
 					};
 				};
+
+				edp0_out: port@1 {
+					reg = <1>;
+
+					edp0_out_con: endpoint {
+						remote-endpoint = <&usb_con_sbu>;
+					};
+				};
 			};
 		};
 
@@ -2570,6 +2585,18 @@
 					 * and is inert here:
 					 * dwc3 only builds a role switch when
 					 * usb-role-switch is set, which it is not.
+					 *
+					 * port@2 is Sideband Use -- "the alternate
+					 * mode connection of which SBU is a part"
+					 * (`ports` schema) --
+					 * and on this board AUXP/AUXN reach SBU1/
+					 * SBU2 with no mux and no redriver between
+					 * (sch p12 L19-44), so the other end of it
+					 * is the DisplayPort transmitter itself.
+					 * It is what lets that transmitter find
+					 * this node and publish a bridge for it,
+					 * which is the only way an out-of-band
+					 * hot-plug can be matched to a connector.
 					 */
 					/*
 					 * Declare that this port itself supports
@@ -2616,6 +2643,13 @@
 								remote-endpoint = <&combophy0_ss>;
 							};
 						};
+
+						port@2 {	/* Sideband Use */
+							reg = <2>;
+							usb_con_sbu: endpoint {
+								remote-endpoint = <&edp0_out_con>;
+							};
+						};
 					};
 				};
 			};
